.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH WALL 1M 
.SH NAME
wall  \-  write to all users
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/wall
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wall
reads its standard input until an end-of-file,
or the named
.I file
if one is given.
It then sends this message,
preceded by
`Broadcast Message ...',
to all logged in users.
.PP
The sender should be super-user to override
any protections the users may have invoked.
.PP
.B Note:
that override is nothing but the ordinary permission check on opening
the terminal, which
.IR mesg (1)
has set to 0600 or 0622.
.I Wall
makes no check of its own \-
unlike
.IR write (1),
which refuses even the super-user on a terminal that has said no.
For the same reason
.I wall
is not set-user-id: the bit would hand the override to everybody
and leave nothing to invoke.
.PP
.B Note:
the file argument is not in the SYNOPSIS of v7's page and has always
been in its program.
Users are served in the slot order of /etc/utmp \-
which
.IR ttyslot (3)
makes the line order of /etc/ttys \-
one forked child apiece, a second apart.
The children are not waited for.
.PP
.B Note:
the message is held in a 3000-character buffer.
v7 read past the end of it without a word; a longer message is
truncated here and says so on the standard error.
.SH FILES
/dev/tty?
.br
/etc/utmp
.br
/etc/ttys
.SH "SEE ALSO"
mesg(1), write(1), who(1), utmp(5)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`cannot open ...' when the open on
a user's tty file fails.
.PP
.B Note:
this page said `Cannot send to ...'; the program has always written
`cannot open'.
